  },
  "articleBody": "Prepping your home for sale or rent can feel overwhelming, and you're probably wondering what's truly worth fixing. The truth is, buyers and renters are looking for a safe, well-maintained space. You'll want to prioritize any safety or structural issues first. Think about things like leaky roofs, faulty electrical systems, or foundation problems—these are big red flags that will almost certainly come up during an inspection and can kill a deal. After that, focus on visible maintenance and general cleanup. You don't necessarily need to remodel your entire kitchen, but fresh paint, clean carpets, and minor repairs to things like leaky faucets or broken light fixtures can make a huge difference in a buyer's first impression and perceived value. It's often helpful to get an inspection done yourself so you know exactly what you're dealing with before you even list. Keep all your repair records and receipts; they demonstrate transparency and can be a big selling point. When in doubt, a professional can help you decide which repairs offer the best return on investment and ensure everything meets local standards.",
